INTERACTION LOGIC (React State & DOM Events)

[ A. State Variables ]

baseIndex: The ID of the image currently locked as the background.

maskIndex: The ID of the image revealed by the flashlight hover.

isLiquidAnimating: Boolean to block interactions during the click transition.

[ B. The Hover & Idle Mechanic (Lando Norris + Re-roll) ]

The mouse tracks normally, mapping --x and --y to a circular CSS clip-path
or Canvas mask to act as the flashlight.

The Idle Timer:

Inside onMouseMove, clear an existing timeout, then set a new timeout
for 600ms.

If the user keeps scrubbing, the timer never fires, and maskIndex stays
locked (continuous background).

If the user STOPS moving their mouse for 600ms inside the container, the
timer fires: trigger a fast CSS opacity fade on the mask layer, randomize
the maskIndex, and fade it back in.

[ C. The Liquid Click (Lock-In) ]

Trigger: User clicks inside the Avatar container.

The Shader/Effect:

We do not just snap the image. We apply a WebGL ripple shader (or an SVG
<feDisplacementMap> mapped to a radial wave).

The epicenter of the ripple is exactly e.nativeEvent.offsetX and offsetY
(where they clicked, not the center of the image).

The Sequence:

isLiquidAnimating = true (disables the hover mask tracking temporarily).

The liquid ripple expands outward from the cursor. As it expands, the
maskIndex image visually overwrites the baseIndex image.

Once the ripple hits the edges (approx 0.8s), setBaseIndex(maskIndex).

Reactivate the hover tracking, and immediately randomize the maskIndex
so the flashlight holds a new style.

TECHNICAL EXPLANATION & IMPLEMENTATION NOTES

Why use an Idle Timer for the randomization instead of randomizing on every frame?
If you randomize the mask image while the mouse is moving, you get a chaotic,
strobe-light flickering effect that is harsh on the eyes and looks broken.
By using a setTimeout debounce in your mouse tracker, you enforce intentionality.
The user gets to "paint" and explore the ASCII texture smoothly. When they pause
to look at it, the system surprises them by shifting to the Depth-Dots.

How to build the Liquid Click without heavy WebGL:
If you want to avoid writing a custom Three.js shader just for this button,
you can achieve the liquid reveal using CSS and SVG.

Create an SVG <filter> with <feTurbulence> (for water noise) and
<feDisplacementMap>.

On click, spawn a temporary div that holds the new image, perfectly aligned
over the base image.

Apply clip-path: circle(0px at X Y) to this new div, and animate the radius
to 150% (covering the whole box).

Apply the SVG liquid displacement filter to the edge of that expanding circle.
This perfectly mimics dropping a stone into water and having the new image
ripple outward.